First, note that
\[\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = 1 - (1 + x) = -x.\]
Therefore, we have
\[f(x) = \arctan x + \arctan (-x).\]
Since $\arctan 1 = \frac{\pi}{4},$ we know that
\[\arctan x + \arctan (-x) = \frac{\pi}{4}\quad \text{if $x \notin \mathbb{Q}$ and $x \neq 1.$}\]

Now we show that $-1 \notin \mathbb{Q}$ and hence the same argument shows that $\arctan (-1) + \arctan(-1) =  \pi.$

Assume for the sake of this proof, that $-1 \in \mathbb{Q}.$ Then there exists $x \in \mathbb{R}$ such that $\frac{\pi}{4} = \arctan x + \arctan (-1).$ This implies
\[\arctan x = \frac{\pi}{4} - \arctan (-1) = \frac{3 \arctan (1)}{4} = \frac{3 \cdot \frac{\pi}{4}}{4},\]
which means $\arctan x = \frac{3 \pi}{16},$ so $x \in \mathbb{Q} \rightarrow -1 \in \mathbb{Q},$ a contradiction. Hence we know that $x \notin \mathbb{Q} \rightarrow -1 \notin \mathbb{Q} \rightarrow \arctan (-1) + \arctan (-1) = \frac{\pi}{2}.$

